{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-aa9938b97953d450", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tLANGUAGE-SPECIALIZED MULTI-TEACHER ON-POLICY DISTILLATION FOR\n2\tMULTILINGUAL LLM-BASED ASR\n3\tYuan Xie∗ , Jiaqi Song, Xianliang Wang, Ming Lei, Jie Gao, Jie Wu\n4\tAdvanced Intelligent Systems Group, NIO, Beijing, China\n5\tryan.xie2@nio.com\n6\t\n7\tarXiv:2608.03610v1 [cs.CL] 4 Aug 2026\n8\t\n9\tABSTRACT\n10\tModern LLM-based ASR systems have established multilingual capability as a standard feature, leveraging large-scale\n11\tmultilingual corpora and LLMs’ cross-lingual knowledge to\n12\tachieve competitive performance across multilingual benchmarks. However, joint modeling of languages with heterogeneous acoustic, phonological, and lexical characteristics\n13\tinevitably introduces optimization conflicts, undermining\n14\tlanguage-wise specialization. To address this challenge, we\n15\tpropose Language-Specialized Multi-Teacher On-Policy\n16\tDistillation (LS-MOPD), which decouples language-specific\n17\tknowledge acquisition from multilingual capability integration: language-specialized teachers are independently optimized via reinforcement learning (RL), after which their\n18\texpertise is integrated into a generalist multilingual student\n19\tthrough language routing and token-level multi-teacher distillation, thereby reducing direct cross-lingual optimization\n20\tconflicts. We further explore two acoustic-prefix configurations, static and dynamic, to examine how teacher–student\n21\tprefix consistency influences the efficacy of on-policy distillation. Experiments on benchmarks covering Mandarin,\n22\tMandarin subdialects, Cantonese, and English demonstrate\n23\tthat LS-MOPD substantially outperforms RL baselines and\n24\tconsistently surpasses the empirical performance envelope\n25\tdefined by best-performing RL teachers, revealing its potential to generalize beyond all teachers in multilingual ASR.\n26\tIndex Terms— multilingual ASR, speech large language\n27\tmodels, multi-teacher on-policy distillation\n28\t1. INTRODUCTION\n29\tLLM-based ASR has rapidly advanced as the mainstream\n30\tparadigm in speech recognition, capitalizing on the extensive\n31\tmodel capacity and rich linguistic priors of pretrained LLMs\n32\tto achieve impressive performance across dozens of languages and dialects. Frontier systems such as Seed-ASR [1],\n33\tFun-ASR [2], and Qwen3-ASR [3] primarily rely on scaling up multilingual corpora, consolidating broad language\n34\tcapabilities into a generalist model in a data-driven manner.\n35\t∗ Corresponding author.\n36\t\n37\tDespite advances on multilingual ASR benchmarks, consolidating cross-lingual capabilities within a generalist model\n38\tremains challenging. Languages and dialects differ systematically in their phonological, acoustic, and linguistic properties. For instance, Mandarin is characterized by pervasive\n39\thomophony and thus relies heavily on contextual semantic disambiguation, whereas English exhibits considerable\n40\tvariability in pronunciation and prosody, demanding precise\n41\tmodeling of stress-timed rhythm and connected-speech phenomena such as phonetic reduction and cross-word linking.\n42\tJointly modeling such heterogeneous languages inevitably introduces gradient conflicts, leading to performance trade-offs\n43\tacross languages. This challenge is amplified in real-time\n44\tASR systems, where stringent latency constraints limit model\n45\tscale, requiring heterogeneous multilingual capabilities to be\n46\taccommodated within a finite model capacity budget.\n47\tRecent advances in multi-teacher on-policy distillation\n48\t(MOPD) [4, 5] offer a principled remedy to this challenge.\n49\tMOPD first trains a set of task-specialized teachers and distills their expertise into a student through task-routed supervision on the student’s on-policy rollouts [6, 7]. In multilingual\n50\tASR, task routing can be naturally adapted to language routing, decoupling language-specific optimization from multilingual integration. Heterogeneous language-wise objectives are independently optimized by specialized teachers\n51\tand then distilled into the student, mitigating direct crosslingual optimization conflicts. However, extending MOPD\n52\tto LLM-based ASR raises several previously unexplored issues. Unlike text-only LLMs, LLM-based ASR conditions\n53\tdecoding on continuous acoustic prefixes produced by the\n54\tencoder–adaptor. The teacher–student prefix consistency depends on the teacher training strategy and may directly affect\n55\tdistillation efficacy. Moreover, cross-lingual heterogeneity\n56\tis generally less pronounced than cross-task heterogeneity, leaving it unclear whether aggregating supervision from\n57\tmultiple language-specialized teachers can yield genuinely\n58\tcomplementary gains in multilingual ASR.\n59\tTo address these issues, we compare alternative MOPD\n60\tvariants and analyze their behavior from the perspectives\n61\tof gradient alignment and multi-teacher complementarity.\n62\tBased on these insights, we propose Language-Specialized\n63\tMulti-Teacher On-Policy Distillation (LS-MOPD). Specif-\n64\t\n65\t\f1. Generate\n66\t\n67\tStudent Model\n68\t(Encoder + adaptor)\n69\t\n70\tStudent rollout\n71\t\n72\tprompt embeddings\n73\t\n74\t......\n75\tToken 1 Token 2\n76\tStudent Model\n77\t(LLM decoder)\n78\t\n79\t3. Optimization\n80\t\n81\tToken n\n82\t\n83\tReverse KL\n84\t\n85\tstatic mode\n86\tdynamic mode\n87\tunified\n88\t\n89\tStudent rollout\n90\t\n91\t1. Generate\n92\t\n93\t......\n94\tToken 1 Token 2\n95\t\n96\tTeacher Model m\n97\t(Encoder + adaptor)\n98\t\n99\tprompt embeddings\n100\t\n101\tTeacher Model 1\n102\t(LLM decoder)\n103\t\n104\t...\n105\t\n106\tToken n\n107\t\n108\t...\n109\t\n110\t......\n111\tToken 1 Token 2\n112\t\n113\tTop-1\n114\trouting\n115\t\n116\tprompt embeddings\n117\t\n118\t...\n119\t\n120\tTeacher supervision\n121\tText prompt\n122\t\n123\tDynamic acoustic prefix\n124\tTeacher Model 1\n125\t(Encoder + adaptor)\n126\t\n127\tStudent Model\n128\t(LLM decoder)\n129\t\n130\tStatic acoustic prefix:\n131\treuse student's\n132\tembeddings\n133\t\n134\t2. Prefill\n135\t\n136\tTeacher\n137\tModel\n138\tTeacher\n139\tModel\n140\tTeacher\n141\tModel\n142\t【LLM\n143\tdecoder】\n144\t【LLM\n145\t(LLMdecoder】\n146\tdecoder)\n147\t\n148\t3. Optimization\n149\t\n150\tOptimization only when using\n151\tdynamic acoustic prefix\n152\t\n153\tInput speech\n154\t\n155\tTeacher Model m\n156\t(LLM decoder)\n157\t\n158\tToken n\n159\t\n160\t2. Prefill\n161\tTeacher 1 supervision\n162\tReverse KL\n163\t\n164\t......\n165\tToken 1 Token 2\n166\t\n167\tToken n\n168\t\n169\tTeacher m supervision\n170\t......\n171\tToken 1 Token 2\n172\t\n173\tWeighted Sum\n174\tReverse KL\n175\t\n176\tToken n\n177\t\n178\t(active only with a dynamic acoustic prefix)\n179\t\n180\t(a) Standard text-based MOPD\n181\t\n182\t(b) LS-MOPD with Dynamic / Static acoustic prefix\n183\t\n184\tFig. 1. Comparison of (a) standard text-based MOPD and (b) LS-MOPD for ASR under two acoustic-prefix configurations.\n185\tIn the dynamic configuration, the student and teachers use independently optimized encoder–adaptors and thus obtain modelspecific acoustic prefixes. In the static configuration, all models share a frozen encoder–adaptor and therefore allow teachers to\n186\treuse the student’s acoustic prefixes. Both configurations support weighted multi-teacher supervision.\n187\tically, we first train language-specialized teachers using the\n188\tDecoupled Clip and Dynamic Sampling Policy Optimization\n189\t(DAPO) algorithm [8] with an ASR-specific reward. During\n190\tdistillation, each student-generated on-policy rollout is routed\n191\tto top-K teachers for the corresponding input-language category. The token-level supervision signals are then weighted\n192\tand aggregated to distill complementary expertise into the\n193\tgeneralist student. Our contributions are as follows:\n194\t• As the first study to explore MOPD for ASR, we\n195\tpresent a systematic framework comprising DAPObased teacher optimization, acoustic-prefix configurations, and multi-teacher weighted aggregation, offering\n196\tpractical guidance for effective distillation.\n197\t• We validate the effectiveness of LS-MOPD through\n198\tanalyses of gradient alignment and multi-teacher complementarity across distinct configurations.\n199\t• We empirically demonstrate the advantages of LSMOPD over RL baselines and its potential to generalize\n200\tbeyond all teachers.\n201\t2. RELATED WORK\n202\tMultilingual ASR. Extending multilingual capability has\n203\tbecome a major focus of modern ASR systems, with existing efforts following two main directions. The first leverages industrial-scale data expansion: systems such as SeedASR [1], Fun-ASR [2], Qwen3-ASR [3], FireRedASR2S [9],\n204\tand NIM4-ASR [10] achieve strong multilingual performance by combining large-scale multilingual speech corpora\n205\twith LLMs’ cross-lingual priors. However, phonological\n206\tand lexical heterogeneity across languages generally demands greater model capacity, whereas real-time ASR systems must operate under stringent latency and computational\n207\tconstraints. This tension is reflected in deployment practice, where providers release multilingual-optimized variants,\n208\t\n209\tsuch as Seed-ASR (ML) [1] and Fun-ASR-MLT-nano [2],\n210\talongside their flagship models. The second direction introduces language-specific architectural mechanisms, such\n211\tas language-aware adaptors, mixture-of-experts routing, and\n212\tgated query banks [11, 12, 13]. While these methods can\n213\timprove multilingual performance at lower cost, they do not\n214\tdirectly enhance the model’s underlying acoustic or semantic modeling capacity and may incur additional deployment\n215\toverhead under high-concurrency inference.\n216\tMulti-teacher on-policy distillation. On-policy distillation\n217\t(OPD) [14] has recently emerged as an effective LLM posttraining algorithm, providing token-level supervision on the\n218\tstudent’s sampled trajectories to mitigate exposure mismatch.\n219\tThis property makes OPD well suited to LLM-based ASR\n220\tsystems, which perform autoregressive generation yet require strict token-level transcription accuracy. Multi-teacher\n221\tOPD [4, 5] extends this idea by independently optimizing\n222\tmultiple domain-specialized teachers and distilling them into\n223\tthe student model with reverse-KL-style objectives, making\n224\tit suitable for capability integration. This is particularly appealing for multilingual ASR, as different languages rely on\n225\tdistinct acoustic-phonetic perception and lexical-semantic\n226\tmodeling capabilities. To the best of our knowledge, we are\n227\tthe first to introduce MOPD into the ASR domain.\n228\t3. METHODS\n229\t3.1. Language-Specialized RL Teacher Training\n230\tPrior to conducting MOPD, we construct a suite of teacher\n231\tmodels specialized for distinct language categories. Starting\n232\tfrom a base model πθ0 built upon the encoder–adaptor–LLM\n233\tarchitecture, we perform reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) based on DAPO [8]. For a given utterance x with reference transcription y, the rollout policy πθold\n234\tsamples G = 8 hypotheses, {τi }G\n235\ti=1 ∼ πθold (· | x). Fol-\n236\t\n237\t\fJDAPO (θ) = E \n238\t\n239\ti=1\n240\t\n241\twhere ri,t = πθ (τi,t | x, τi,<t )/πθold (τi,t | x, τi,<t ) is the\n242\ttoken-level importance ratio, and rei,t = clip(ri,t , 1−εlow , 1+\n243\tεhigh ) is its asymmetrically clipped counterpart. Here, εlow\n244\tand εhigh denote the clipping margins. Compared with classic RLVR objectives, DAPO uses a token-level objective to\n245\tbalance contributions across transcription lengths and prevent\n246\terrors in long utterances from being underweighted. Its dynamic sampling strategy further improves efficiency by resampling informative instances.\n247\tWe construct a fixed multilingual pool of 50k utterances:\n248\t20k Mandarin, 10k Mandarin subdialects, 10k Cantonese, and\n249\t10k English samples. We group these data into three routing\n250\tcategories: Mandarin, Chinese dialects (Mandarin subdialects and Cantonese), and English. Each specialist teacher\n251\tis trained on all data from its target routing category together\n252\twith a randomly sampled 20% subset from each of the other\n253\trouting categories, yielding three language-specialized teachers. In addition, a generalist teacher is trained on the full\n254\tpool. For controlled comparison, DAPO training varies the\n255\tacoustic front end along two orthogonal dimensions: encoder\n256\tmode (streaming vs. offline) and encoder-adaptor trainability (frozen vs. trainable), resulting in four configurations:\n257\t{streaming, offline} × {frozen, trainable}.\n258\t3.2. Language-Routed Multi-teacher OPD\n259\tAfter obtaining the three language-specialized teachers and\n260\tthe generalist teacher, we assess all teachers on held-out validation sets and rank them separately for Mandarin, Chinese\n261\tdialects (including Mandarin subdialects and Cantonese), and\n262\tEnglish. Let πϕTℓ(x),k denote the k-th ranked teacher for routing category ℓ(x), where k = 1 denotes the best-performing\n263\tteacher. The MOPD student πθ is initialized from the base\n264\tmodel πθ0 . Following MOPD [5], the student generates an\n265\ton-policy trajectory τ ∼ πθ (· | x). Let st = (x, τ<t ) denote\n266\t\n267\t(3)\n268\tand the final MOPD objective aggregates contributions from\n269\tthe top-K teachers (K = 3 by default):\n270\t\"K\n271\t#\n272\tK\n273\tX\n274\tX\n275\tLMOPD (θ) = Ex∼D\n276\tλk Lk (x; θ) ,\n277\tλk = 1. (4)\n278\tk=1\n279\t\n280\tk=1\n281\t\n282\tThe weights {λk } govern the relative influence of each\n283\tranked teacher, while setting λk = 0 allows the effective\n284\tnumber of active teachers to range from three to one. The\n285\tformulation recovers top-1 teacher distillation (see Fig. 1\n286\t(a)) by setting (λ1 , λ2 , λ3 ) = (1, 0, 0), while also supporting\n287\tweighted multi-teacher aggregation such as (0.6, 0.2, 0.2).\n288\tImportantly, the supervising teacher πϕTℓ(x),k is determined\n289\taccording to the evaluation performance of all candidate\n290\tteachers for the language category ℓ(x). This ensures that\n291\teach student trajectory can be supervised by the empirically\n292\tstrongest teachers for its target language category.\n293\t3.3. Preliminary Analysis on the Effect of Acoustic Prefix\n294\t0.9550\n295\t\n296\t92\n297\t\n298\t0.82\n299\t\n300\t0.9500\n301\t\n302\t91\n303\t\n304\t0.80\n305\t\n306\t0.9475\n307\t\n308\t90\n309\t\n310\t0.78\n311\t\n312\t0.9450\n313\tStatic prefix (Frozen)\n314\tDynamic prefix (Trainable)\n315\t\n316\t0.9425\n317\t0.9400\n318\t\n319\t93\n320\t\n321\t0.84\n322\t\n323\t0.9525\n324\t\n325\t0\n326\t\n327\t2\n328\t\n329\t4\n330\t\n331\t89\n332\t\n333\t0.76\n334\t\n335\t6\n336\t\n337\tTraining step (×10³)\n338\t(a) Train reward during teacher training\n339\t\n340\tTokens with a positive OPD–CE\n341\tgradient dot product (%) ↑\n342\t\n343\t\u0010\n344\t\u0011\n345\tbi , rei,t A\n346\tbi\n347\tmin\n348\tr\n349\tA\n350\ti,t\n351\tt=1\n352\t , (2)\n353\tPG\n354\ti=1 |τi |\n355\t\n356\t PG P|τ |\n357\ti\n358\t\n359\t\n360\t|τ |\n361\t\u0011\n362\t\u0010\n363\tX\n364\t1\n365\tLk (x; θ) = Eτ ∼πθ \n366\tDKL πθ (· | st ) ∥ πϕTℓ(x),k (· | st )  ,\n367\t|τ | t=1\n368\t\n369\t\n370\tMean cosine similarity between\n371\tOPD and CE logit gradients ↑\n372\t\n373\twhere ℓ(x) denotes the language category of the input and\n374\tERℓ(x) denotes the language-dependent transcription error\n375\trate, instantiated as character error rate (CER) for Chinese and\n376\tword error rate (WER) for English. The group-normalized\n377\tbi = (Ri − R)/(σR + ε),\n378\tadvantage estimate is given by A\n379\twhere R and σR are the mean and standard deviation of the\n380\trewards within the rollout group, respectively. Groups with\n381\tσR = 0 are discarded through dynamic sampling. DAPO\n382\tthen optimizes the following policy-gradient objective:\n383\t\n384\tthe decoding state at step t. The distillation loss from the k-th\n385\tranked teacher is defined as\n386\t\n387\tTrain reward ↑\n388\t\n389\tlowing the reward design adopted in [10], each hypothesis is\n390\tscored by an accuracy reward:\n391\t\u0002\n392\t\u0003\n393\tRi = Racc (τi , y) = exp −2 · ERℓ(x) (τi , y) ,\n394\t(1)\n395\t\n396\tTop-1\n397\t\n398\tTop-2\n399\t\n400\tTop-3\n401\t\n402\t88\n403\t\n404\tNumber of teachers in the OPD target\n405\t(b) OPD supervision aligns with the GT direction\n406\t\n407\tFig. 2. Comparison of static and dynamic acoustic-prefix configurations in terms of (a) accuracy reward trajectories during\n408\tRL and (b) OPD–CE gradient alignment metrics during distillation. Error bars are estimated from 10k bootstrap resamples.\n409\tWhen adapting MOPD to ASR, the LLM is conditioned\n410\ton acoustic embeddings that serve as acoustic prefixes, whose\n411\tconfiguration directly influences distillation effectiveness. A\n412\tstatic acoustic-prefix configuration ensures prefix consistency\n413\tbetween teachers and the student, mitigating the teacher–\n414\tstudent conditioning mismatch during distillation. However,\n415\tthis configuration requires the teacher and student to share\n416\ta frozen encoder–adaptor throughout RL and OPD, which\n417\tlimits the attainable performance of teachers. In contrast,\n418\ta dynamic configuration allows unrestricted joint optimization of all model components, but at the risk of increased\n419\tconditioning inconsistency.\n420\tTo illustrate this trade-off, we conduct a preliminary analysis under static and dynamic acoustic-prefix configurations.\n421\t\n422\t\fTable 1. Offline ASR performance measured by CER (%) for\n423\tChinese and WER (%) for English (lower is better). Model\n424\tsizes are shown in brackets.\n425\tWeNet KeSpeec