t1.17 | 2.48\n545\t1.15 | 2.51\n546\t\n547\t4.63\n548\t4.55\n549\t4.49\n550\t4.46\n551\t4.48\n552\t\n553\tNote: Boldface separately indicates the best result among “Advanced\n554\tOpen-source Models” and among “Our Methods” for each benchmark.\n555\t\n556\tAs shown in Fig. 2, we compare (a) the accuracy-reward\n557\ttrajectories during RL training and (b) token-level alignment\n558\tbetween the OPD gradients and the cross-entropy (CE) gradients derived from ground-truth labels during distillation.\n559\tThe latter is quantified by the mean cosine similarity and\n560\tthe proportion of directionally aligned tokens, defined as\n561\tthose with a positive OPD–CE gradient dot product. The\n562\tresults clearly show that the static prefix yields slightly lower\n563\tteacher performance but better-aligned distillation gradients,\n564\twhereas the dynamic prefix improves teacher performance\n565\tat the expense of slightly reduced gradient alignment. This\n566\treveals a trade-off between teacher strength and distillation\n567\tcompatibility, further examined in the following section.\n568\t\n569\tTable 2. Streaming ASR performance measured by CER (%)\n570\tfor Chinese and WER (%) for English (lower is better).\n571\tMethod\n572\tBase Model (NIM4-ASR)\n573\twith static acoustic prefix\n574\tRL (Generalist Teacher)\n575\tRL (Best-Teacher Oracle)\n576\tLS-MOPD with 1 teacher\n577\tLS-MOPD with 2 teachers\n578\tLS-MOPD with 3 teachers\n579\twith dynamic acoustic prefix\n580\tRL (Generalist Teacher)\n581\tRL (Best-Teacher Oracle)\n582\tLS-MOPD with 1 teacher\n583\tLS-MOPD with 2 teachers\n584\tLS-MOPD with 3 teachers\n585\t\n586\tWeNet KeSpeech WeNet-Yue LibriSpeech\n587\tAvg.\n588\tmeet. | net\n589\ttest\n590\tshort | long clean | other\n591\t6.19 | 5.26\n592\t\n593\t5.69\n594\t\n595\t6.06 | 12.33 1.34 | 3.32\n596\t\n597\t5.74\n598\t\n599\t5.92 | 5.19\n600\t5.84 | 5.18\n601\t5.76 | 5.00\n602\t5.67 | 4.99\n603\t5.69 | 4.96\n604\t\n605\t5.54\n606\t5.54\n607\t5.12\n608\t5.17\n609\t5.09\n610\t\n611\t5.68 | 11.10\n612\t5.50 | 11.10\n613\t5.61 | 10.83\n614\t5.59 | 10.67\n615\t5.52 | 10.61\n616\t\n617\t1.29 | 3.27\n618\t1.25 | 3.20\n619\t1.25 | 3.21\n620\t1.24 | 3.15\n621\t1.23 | 3.19\n622\t\n623\t5.43\n624\t5.37\n625\t5.25\n626\t5.21\n627\t5.18\n628\t\n629\t5.90 | 5.26\n630\t5.72 | 5.26\n631\t5.65 | 5.17\n632\t5.71 | 5.16\n633\t5.78 | 5.26\n634\t\n635\t5.52\n636\t5.50\n637\t5.30\n638\t5.24\n639\t5.22\n640\t\n641\t5.60 | 11.10\n642\t5.58 | 10.96\n643\t5.58 | 11.19\n644\t5.99 | 11.02\n645\t5.51 | 10.90\n646\t\n647\t1.35 | 3.22\n648\t1.31 | 3.16\n649\t1.29 | 3.17\n650\t1.27 | 3.12\n651\t1.28 | 3.15\n652\t\n653\t5.42\n654\t5.36\n655\t5.34\n656\t5.36\n657\t5.30\n658\t\n659\taforementioned four datasets, comprising seven subsets in\n660\ttotal. Across all evaluations, a unified text normalization\n661\tpipeline based on WenetTextProcess is applied to mitigate\n662\tthe effects of surface-form variations. All experiments adopt\n663\tdeterministic decoding strategies. In the streaming setting,\n664\tspeech is encoded in 640 ms chunks, with four preceding\n665\tchunks retained as left context, and greedy decoding (beam\n666\tsize = 1) is employed. In the offline setting, the encoder operates over the complete utterance, and beam search decoding\n667\tis applied with a beam size of 3.\n668\t4.2. Training Details\n669\tFor RL training, we train for at most 6k optimization steps\n670\twith a maximum learning rate of 2 × 10−6 linearly decayed\n671\tto zero, and a sampling temperature that is cosine-annealed\n672\tfrom 1.0 to 0.7. For MOPD, we train for 20k steps using a\n673\tmaximum learning rate of 4 × 10−6 cosine-annealed to 2 ×\n674\t10−6 and a fixed sampling temperature of 1.0. All RL and\n675\tMOPD experiments are conducted on 8 NVIDIA A100 GPUs\n676\twith BF16 precision and DeepSpeed ZeRO-2 [22].\n677\t\n678\t4. EXPERIMENTS\n679\t4.3. Main Results\n680\t4.1. Experimental Setup\n681\tThe base student model in this work is built upon an inhouse backbone pretrained on approximately 560k hours of\n682\tlabeled speech, comprising a 0.6B FireRed Conformer [9], a\n683\t4× downsampling linear adaptor, and a Qwen3-1.7B LLM\n684\tdecoder. For RL and MOPD, we construct a 50k-utterance\n685\tmultilingual training pool by sampling from representative\n686\tdatasets: 20k Mandarin utterances from WenetSpeech [18],\n687\t10k Mandarin subdialect utterances from KeSpeech [19],\n688\t10k Cantonese utterances from WenetSpeech-Yue [20], and\n689\t10k English utterances from LibriSpeech [21]. The generalist teacher and MOPD stages use the full multilingual pool,\n690\twhereas language-specialized teachers are trained on their respective language-skewed subsets constructed with sampling\n691\tratios specified in Section 3.1.\n692\tFor evaluation, we employ the official test sets of the\n693\t\n694\tTables 1 and 2 report the performance of our proposed methods on benchmarks, alongside leading open-source models\n695\tas references for the current state of the art. Within each\n696\tacoustic-prefix configuration, the generalist teacher serves as\n697\tthe RL baseline, whereas the best result attained by any candidate teacher on each benchmark is reported as “RL (BestTeacher Oracle)”. For LS-MOPD, the number of supervising teachers is determined by the loss weights in Eq. (4):\n698\tthe one-, two-, and three-teacher variants use LMOPD = L1 ,\n699\tLMOPD = 0.6L1 + 0.4L2 , and LMOPD = 0.6L1 + 0.2L2 +\n700\t0.2L3 , respectively. We analyze the results as follows:\n701\t(1) Overall Performance. Using only a 50k-utterance\n702\tpost-training pool, LS-MOPD substantially improves upon\n703\tthe base model and consistently outperforms the RL baseline.\n704\tThe average error rate drops to a minimum of 4.45%, outperforming all open-source baselines, including models trained\n705\t\n706\t\f4.4. Sources of Improvement and Emergent Gains\n707\tBeyond benchmark results, we conduct visual analyses to validate LS-MOPD and offer insight into its emergent behavior.\n708\tAs shown in Fig. 3 (a), we examine the relationship between the benchmark gains of different LS-MOPD variants\n709\tand the proportion of tokens with aligned OPD and CE gradients, as defined in Section 3.3. The results show a positive\n710\tassociation: static-prefix LS-MOPD variants exhibit more\n711\tconsistently aligned gradients and achieve larger performance\n712\tgains. By contrast, although dynamic-prefix teachers show\n713\tstronger standalone performance, their supervision induces\n714\tgradients that are less aligned with the CE gradients, leading\n715\tto smaller distillation gains. Moreover, multi-teacher supervision generally yields better gradient alignment than singleteacher supervision. This finding provides a preliminary\n716\t\n717\t0.15\n718\t\n719\tStatic prefix (Frozen)\n720\tDynamic prefix (Trainable)\n721\t\n722\t0.10\n723\t0.05\n724\t0.00\n725\t\n726\tTop-3\n727\t\n728\tTop-3\n729\tTop-2\n730\t\n731\tTop-1\n732\t\n733\tTop-1\n734\tTop-2\n735\t90.0\n736\t\n737\t90.5\n738\t\n739\t91.0\n740\t\n741\t91.5\n742\t\n743\tStudent correct while all teachers\n744\tare wrong (% of reference tokens) ↑\n745\t\n746\tError-rate reduction relative to\n747\tthe dataset-best teacher (points) ↑\n748\t\n749\ton million-hour-scale speech corpora [2, 3] and those built\n750\tupon larger LLM backbones [15, 16, 17], using only 2.3B parameters. Notably, LS-MOPD surpasses the empirical performance envelope defined by the best-teacher oracle on almost\n751\tall benchmarks, and achieves the best results among all compared leading systems on WeNetSpeech-Yue-short and LibriSpeech test-clean. These results demonstrate that the distilled student can outperform all teachers, achieving highly\n752\tcompetitive multilingual recognition accuracy together with\n753\tmeasurable emergent gains. As shown by the streaming results in Table 2, LS-MOPD also delivers substantial gains in\n754\tthe streaming setting, with relative improvements exceeding\n755\tthose observed in the offline configuration.\n756\t(2) Static vs. dynamic acoustic prefix. Before distillation, dynamic-prefix RL teachers achieve slightly better\n757\taverage performance than their static-prefix counterparts,\n758\towing to joint encoder–adaptor optimization that yields more\n759\trefined acoustic representations. After MOPD, however,\n760\tstatic-prefix variants attain the best average results in both the\n761\toffline and streaming settings. We attribute this reversal to\n762\tteacher–student acoustic-prefix mismatch under the dynamic\n763\tconfiguration: when teacher supervision depends on acoustic cues absent from the student’s representation, distillation\n764\tmay shift from learnable preference transfer toward fitting\n765\tpartially unreachable targets.\n766\t(3) Top-K teacher weighted aggregation. Since crosslingual heterogeneity is generally less pronounced than crosstask heterogeneity, we extend the default top-1 routing of\n767\tMOPD to weighted top-K teacher aggregation. This yields\n768\tmodest gains, particularly in the streaming setting, suggesting a beneficial regularization effect that becomes more pronounced under higher decoding uncertainty. Notably, we\n769\tfind that incorporating the second- and third-ranked teachers\n770\tcan lead to improvements despite their substantially lower\n771\tstandalone performance than the best teacher, suggesting that\n772\tteacher diversity can provide complementary supervision and\n773\talleviate token-level overconfidence.\n774\t\n775\tTokens with a positive OPD–CE gradient dot product (%) ↑\n776\t\n777\t0.30\n778\t0.25\n779\t0.20\n780\t0.15\n781\t0.10\n782\t0.05\n783\t0.00\n784\tTop-1\n785\t\n786\tTop-2\n787\t\n788\tTop-3\n789\t\n790\tNumber of teachers in the OPD target\n791\t\n792\t(a) Helpful-gradient prevalence is associated with error-rate gain\n793\t\n794\t(b) Student-only correct positions\n795\t\n796\tFig. 3. (a) Relationship between OPD–CE gradient alignment\n797\tand the performance gains achieved by LS-MOPD. (b) Emergent gains: the proportion of tokens incorrectly predicted by\n798\tall teachers but correctly predicted by the student.\n799\tmechanistic explanation for why multi-teacher distillation\n800\tcan outperform its single-teacher counterpart: lower-ranked\n801\tteachers can still contribute complementary supervision that\n802\tinduces more favorable optimization directions.\n803\tFig. 3 (b) further reveals the emergent benefit of LSMOPD: a non-negligible proportion of tokens are predicted\n804\tincorrectly by all teachers, yet are correctly recognized by the\n805\tstudent. Together with the consistent improvements of LSMOPD over the best-teacher oracle in Tables 1 and 2, these\n806\tresults highlight the potential of LS-MOPD for multilingual\n807\tASR, demonstrating that coordinated supervision from multiple teachers can enable the student to generalize beyond the\n808\tperformance envelope of any individual teacher.\n809\t5. CONCLUSION\n810\tThis work proposes a multi-teacher on-policy distillation\n811\tframework for multilingual LLM-based ASR. We systematically investigate the impact of static and dynamic acousticprefix configurations and integrate language-specialized\n812\tteachers through language-routed, weighted supervision. Experimental results across both offline and streaming scenarios\n813\tdemonstrate that LS-MOPD substantially outperforms RL\n814\tbaselines and has the potential to generalize beyond the empirical upper envelope of trained RL teachers. We further\n815\tanalyze the effectiveness of LS-MOPD from the perspectives\n816\tof gradient alignment and multi-teacher complementarity,\n817\tand derive practical insights into acoustic-prefix selection\n818\tand multi-teacher aggregation.\n819\tOur work also has several limitations. First, the evaluation\n820\tis relatively narrow in scope, covering only a single model\n821\tbackbone and a limited set of target languages. Broader validation across model architectures and languages is still warranted. Second, our current dynamic acoustic-prefix configuration remains preliminary and does not yet fully exploit the\n822\tpotential of this design. Future work will explore teacher–\n823\tstudent supervision mechanisms that preserve the optimization flexibility of dynamic acoustic prefixes while reducing\n824\tthe resulting conditioning mismatch.\n825\t\n826\t\f6. REFERENCES\n827\t[1] Ye Bai, Jingping Chen, Jitong Chen, Wei Chen, Zhuo\n828\tChen, Chuang Ding, Linhao Dong, Qianqian Dong, Yujiao Du, Kepan Gao, et al., “Seed-asr: Understanding diverse speech and contexts with llm-based speech recognition,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.04675, 2024.\n829\t[2] Keyu An, Yanni Chen, Zhigao Chen, Chong Deng, Zhihao Du, Changfeng Gao, Zhifu Gao, Bo Gong, Xiangang Li, Yabin Li, et al., “Fun-asr technical report,”\n830\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2509.12508, 2025.\n831\t[3] Xian Shi, Xiong Wang, Zhifang Guo, Yongqi Wang,\n832\tPei Zhang, Xinyu Zhang, Zishan Guo, Hongkun Hao,\n833\tYu Xi, Baosong Yang, et al., “Qwen3-asr technical report,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.21337, 2026.\n834\t[4] Bangjun Xiao, Bingquan Xia, Bo Yang, Bofei Gao,\n835\tBowen Shen, Chen Zhang, Chenhong He, Chiheng Lou,\n836\tFuli Luo, Gang Wang, et al., “Mimo-v2-flash technical\n837\treport,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.02780, 2026.\n838\t[5] Wenhan Ma, Jianyu Wei, Liang Zhao, Hailin Zhang,\n839\tBangjun Xiao, Lei Li, Qibin Yang, Bofei Gao, Yudong\n840\tWang, Rang Li, Jinhao Dong, Zhifang Sui, and Fuli\n841\tLuo, “Mopd: Multi-teacher on-policy distillation for capability integration in llm post-training,” arXiv preprint\n842\tarXiv:2606.30406, 2026.\n843\t[6] Zhuolin Yang, Zihan Liu, Yang Chen, Wenliang Dai,\n844\tBoxin Wang, Sheng-Chieh Lin, Chankyu Lee, Yangyi\n845\tChen, Dongfu Jiang, Jiafan He, et al., “Nemotroncascade 2: Post-training llms with cascade rl and\n846\tmulti-domain on-policy distillation,” arXiv preprint\n847\tarXiv:2603.19220, 2026.\n848\t[7] Anyi Xu, Bangcai Lin, Bing Xue, Bingxuan Wang,\n849\tBingzheng Xu, Bochao Wu, Bowei Zhang, Chaofan\n850\tLin, Chen Dong, Chenchen Ling, et al., “Deepseekv4: Towards highly efficient million-token context intelligence,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.19348, 2026.\n851\t[8] Qiying Yu, Zheng Zhang, Ruofei Zhu, Yufeng Yuan, Xiaochen Zuo, Yu Yue, Weinan Dai, Tiantian Fan, Gaohong Liu, Lingjun Liu, et al., “Dapo: An open-source\n852\tllm reinforcement learning system at scale,” Advances\n853\tin Neural Information Processing Systems, vol. 38, pp.\n854\t113222–113244, 2025.\n855\t[9] Kaituo Xu, Yan Jia, Kai Huang, Junjie Chen, Wenpeng Li, Kun Liu, Feng-Long Xie, Xu Tang, and Yao\n856\tHu, “Fireredasr2s: A state-of-the-art industrial-grade\n857\tall-in-one automatic speech recognition system,” arXiv\n858\tpreprint arXiv:2603.10420, 2026.\n859\t\n860\t[10] Yuan Xie, Jiaqi Song, Guang Qiu, Xianliang Wang, Kai\n861\tQiao, Junfeng Yuan, Shengqing Liu, Yi Zhang, Bowen\n862\tChen, Ming Lei, et al., “Nim4-asr: Towards efficient,\n863\trobust, and customizable real-time llm-based asr,” arXiv\n864\tpreprint arXiv:2604.18105, 2026.\n865\t[11] Junjie Li, Jing Peng, Yangui Fang, Shuai Wang, and Kai\n866\tYu, “Mosa: Mixtures of simple adapters outperform\n867\tmonolithic approaches in llm-based multilingual asr,”\n868\tin 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\n869\tSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2026,\n870\tpp. 18822–18826.\n871\t[12] Guodong Lin, Ziqi Chen, Yuxiang Fu, Ke Li, and WeiQiang Zhang, “Enhancing multilingual llm-based asr\n872\twith mixture of experts and dynamic downsampling,”\n873\tin 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\n874\tSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2026,\n875\tpp. 18807–18811.\n876\t[13] Shreyas Gopal, Donghang Wu, Ashutosh Anshul,\n877\tYeo Yue Heng, Yizhou Peng, Haoyang Li, Hexin Liu,\n878\tand Eng Siong Chng, “Language-aware distillation for\n879\tmultilingual instruction-following speech llms with asronly supervision,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.07025,\n880\t2026.\n881\t[14] Rishabh Agarwal, Nino Vieillard, Yongchao Zhou, Piotr Stanczyk, Sabela Ramos Garea, Matthieu Geist, and\n882\tOlivier Bachem, “On-policy distillation of language\n883\tmodels: Learning from self-generated mistakes,” in\n884\tInternational Conference on Learning Representations,\n885\t2024.\n886\t[15] Boyong Wu, Chao Yan, Chen Hu, Cheng Yi, Chengli\n887\tFeng, Fei Tian, Feiyu Shen, Gang Yu, Haoyang Zhang,\n888\tJingbei Li, et al., “Step-audio 2 technical report,” arXiv\n889\tpreprint arXiv:2507.16632, 2025.\n890\t[16] Jin Xu, Zhifang Guo, Hangrui Hu, Yunfei Chu, Xiong\n891\tWang, Jinzheng He, Yuxuan Wang, Xian Shi, Ting He,\n892\tXinfa Zhu, et al., “Qwen3-omni technical report,” arXiv\n893\tpreprint arXiv:2509.17765, 2025.\n894\t[17] LLM-Core-Team Xiaomi, “Mimo-v2.5-asr: Robust\n895\tspeech recognition across languages, dialects, and complex acoustic scenarios,” 2026.\n896\t[18] Binbin Zhang, Hang Lv, Pengcheng Guo, Qijie Shao,\n897\tChao Yang, Lei Xie, Xin Xu, Hui Bu, Xiaoyu Chen,\n898\tChenchen Zeng, et al., “Wenetspeech: A 10000+ hours\n899\tmulti-domain mandarin corpus for speech recognition,”\n900\tin 2022 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\n901\tSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2022,\n902\tpp. 6182–6186.\n903\t\n904\t\f[19] Zhiyuan Tang, Dong Wang, Yanguang Xu, Jianwei Sun,\n905\tXiaoning Lei, Shuaijiang Zhao, Cheng Wen, Xingjun\n906\tTan, Chuandong Xie, Shuran Zhou, et al., “Kespeech:\n907\tAn open source speech dataset of mandarin and its eight\n908\tsubdialects,” in Thirty-fifth conference on neural information processing systems datasets and benchmarks\n909\ttrack (Round 2), 2021.\n910\t[20] Longhao Li, Zhao Guo, Hongjie Chen, Yuhang Dai,\n911\tZiyu Zhang, Hongfei Xue, Tianlun Zuo, Chengyou\n912\tWang, Shuiyuan Wang, Xin Xu, et al., “Wenetspeechyue: A large-scale cantonese speech corpus with multidimensional annotation,” in Proceedings of the AAAI\n913\tConference on Artificial Intelligence, 2026, vol. 40, pp.\n914\t31627–31635.\n915\t[21] Vassil Panayotov, Guoguo Chen, Daniel Povey, and Sanjeev Khudanpur, “Librispeech: an asr corpus based on\n916\tpublic domain audio books,” in 2015 IEEE international\n917\tconference on acoustics, speech and signal processing\n918\t(ICASSP). IEEE, 2015, pp. 5206–5210.\n919\t[22] Samyam Rajbhandari, Jeff Rasley, Olatunji Ruwase,\n920\tand Yuxiong He, “Zero: Memory optimizations toward\n921\ttraining trillion parameter models,” in SC20: international conference for high performance computing, networking, storage and analysis. IEEE, 2020, pp. 1–16.\n922\t\n923\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d8df3b29-d3d6-4d7b-982e-ed36ef6c1805", "uuid": "c407231c-4619-4c20-acb4-be139ddb1d27", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T05:39:42.571Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608.txt", "content": "LANGUAGE-SPECIALIZED MULTI-TEACHER ON-POLICY DISTILLATION FOR\nMULTILINGUAL LLM-BASED ASR\nYuan Xie∗ , Jiaqi Song, Xianliang Wang, Ming Lei, Jie Gao, Jie Wu\nAdvanced Intelligent Systems Group, NIO, Beijing, China\nryan.xie2@nio.com\n\narXiv:2608.03610v1 [cs.CL] 4 Aug 2026\n\nABSTRACT\nModern LLM-based ASR systems have established multilingual capability as a standard feature, leveraging large-scale\nmultilingual corpora and LLMs’ cross-lingual knowledge to\nachieve competitive performance across multilingual benchmarks. However, joint modeling of languages with heterogeneous acoustic, phonological, and lexical characteristics\ninevitably introduces optimization conflicts, undermining\nlanguage-wise specialization. To address this challenge, we\npropose Language-Specialized Multi-Teacher On-Policy\nDistillation (LS-MOPD), which decouples language-specific\nknowledge acquisition from multilingual capability integration: language-specialized teachers are independently optimized via reinforcement learning (RL), after which their\nexpertise is integrated into a generalist multilingual student\nthrough language routing and token-level multi-teacher distillation, thereby reducing direct cross-lingual optimization\nconflicts. We further explore two acoustic-prefix configurations, static and dynamic, to examine how teacher–student\nprefix consistency influences the efficacy of on-policy distillation. Experiments on benchmarks covering Mandarin,\nMandarin subdialects, Cantonese, and English demonstrate\nthat LS-MOPD substantially outperforms RL baselines and\nconsistently surpasses the empirical performance envelope\ndefined by best-performing RL teachers, revealing its potential to generalize beyond all teachers in multilingual ASR.\nIndex Terms— multilingual ASR, speech large language\nmodels, multi-teacher on-policy distillation\n1. INTRODUCTION\nLLM-based ASR has rapidly advanced as the mainstream\nparadigm in speech recognition, capitalizing on the extensive\nmodel capacity and rich linguistic priors of pretrained LLMs\nto achieve impressive performance across dozens of languages and dialects. Frontier systems such as Seed-ASR [1],\nFun-ASR [2], and Qwen3-ASR [3] primarily rely on scaling up multilingual corpora, consolidating broad language\ncapabilities into a generalist model in a data-driven manner.\n∗ Corresponding author.\n\nDespite advances on multilingual ASR benchmarks, consolidating cross-lingual capabilities within a generalist model\nremains challenging. Languages and dialects differ systematically in their phonological, acoustic, and linguistic properties. For instance, Mandarin is characterized by pervasive\nhomophony and thus relies heavily on contextual semantic disambiguation, whereas English exhibits considerable\nvariability in pronunciation and prosody, demanding precise\nmodeling of stress-timed rhythm and connected-speech phenomena such as phonetic reduction and cross-word linking.\nJointly modeling such heterogeneous languages inevitably introduces gradient conflicts, leading to performance trade-offs\nacross languages. This challenge is amplified in real-time\nASR systems, where stringent latency constraints limit model\nscale, requiring heterogeneous multilingual capabilities to be\naccommodated within a finite model capacity budget.\nRecent advances in multi-teacher on-policy distillation\n(MOPD) [4, 5] offer a principled remedy to this challenge.\nMOPD first trains a set of task-specialized teachers and distills their expertise into a student through task-routed supervision on the student’s on-policy rollouts [6, 7]. In multilingual\nASR, task routing can be naturally adapted to language routing, decoupling language-specific optimization from multilingual integration. Heterogeneous language-wise objectives are independently optimized by specialized teachers\nand then distilled into the student, mitigating direct crosslingual optimization conflicts. However, extending MOPD\nto LLM-based ASR raises several previously unexplored issues. Unlike text-only LLMs, LLM-based ASR conditions\ndecoding on continuous acoustic prefixes produced by the\nencoder–adaptor. The teacher–student prefix consistency depends on the teacher training strategy and may directly affect\ndistillation efficacy. Moreover, cross-lingual heterogeneity\nis generally less pronounced than cross-task heterogeneity, leaving it unclear whether aggregating supervision from\nmultiple language-specialized teachers can yield genuinely\ncomplementary gains in multilingual ASR.\nTo address these issues, we compare alternative MOPD\nvariants and analyze their behavior from the perspectives\nof gradient alignment and multi-teacher complementarity.\nBased on these insights, we propose Language-Specialized\nMulti-Teacher On-Policy Distillation (LS-MOPD). Specif-\n\n\f1. Generate\n\nStudent Model\n(Encoder + adaptor)\n\nStudent rollout\n\nprompt embeddings\n\n......\nToken 1 Token 2\nStudent Model\n(LLM decoder)\n\n3. Optimization\n\nToken n\n\nReverse KL\n\nstatic mode\ndynamic mode\nunified\n\nStudent rollout\n\n1. Generate\n\n......\nToken 1 Token 2\n\nTeacher Model m\n(Encoder + adaptor)\n\nprompt embeddings\n\nTeacher Model 1\n(LLM decoder)\n\n...\n\nToken n\n\n...\n\n......\nToken 1 Token 2\n\nTop-1\nrouting\n\nprompt embeddings\n\n...\n\nTeacher supervision\nText prompt\n\nDynamic acoustic prefix\nTeacher Model 1\n(Encoder + adaptor)\n\nStudent Model\n(LLM decoder)\n\nStatic acoustic prefix:\nreuse student's\nembeddings\n\n2. Prefill\n\nTeacher\nModel\nTeacher\nModel\nTeacher\nModel\n【LLM\ndecoder】\n【LLM\n(LLMdecoder】\ndecoder)\n\n3. Optimization\n\nOptimization only when using\ndynamic acoustic prefix\n\nInput speech\n\nTeacher Model m\n(LLM decoder)\n\nToken n\n\n2. Prefill\nTeacher 1 supervision\nReverse KL\n\n......\nToken 1 Token 2\n\nToken n\n\nTeacher m supervision\n......\nToken 1 Token 2\n\nWeighted Sum\nReverse KL\n\nToken n\n\n(active only with a dynamic acoustic prefix)\n\n(a) Standard text-based MOPD\n\n(b) LS-MOPD with Dynamic / Static acoustic prefix\n\nFig. 1. Comparison of (a) standard text-based MOPD and (b) LS-MOPD for ASR under two acoustic-prefix configurations.\nIn the dynamic configuration, the student and teachers use independently optimized encoder–adaptors and thus obtain modelspecific acoustic prefixes. In the static configuration, all models share a frozen encoder–adaptor and therefore allow teachers to\nreuse the student’s acoustic prefixes. Both configurations support weighted multi-teacher supervision.\nically, we first train language-specialized teachers using the\nDecoupled Clip and Dynamic Sampling Policy Optimization\n(DAPO) algorithm [8] with an ASR-specific reward. During\ndistillation, each student-generated on-policy rollout is routed\nto top-K teachers for the corresponding input-language category. The token-level supervision signals are then weighted\nand aggregated to distill complementary expertise into the\ngeneralist student. Our contributions are as follows:\n• As the first study to explore MOPD for ASR, we\npresent a systematic framework comprising DAPObased teacher optimization, acoustic-prefix configurations, and multi-teacher weighted aggregation, offering\npractical guidance for effective distillation.\n• We validate the effectiveness of LS-MOPD through\nanalyses of gradient alignment and multi-teacher complementarity across distinct configurations.\n• We empirically demonstrate the advantages of LSMOPD over RL baselines and its potential to generalize\nbeyond all teachers.\n2. RELATED WORK\nMultilingual ASR. Extending multilingual capability has\nbecome a major focus of modern ASR systems, with existing efforts following two main directions. The first leverages industrial-scale data expansion: systems such as SeedASR [1], Fun-ASR [2], Qwen3-ASR [3], FireRedASR2S [9],\nand NIM4-ASR [10] achieve strong multilingual performance by combining large-scale multilingual speech corpora\nwith LLMs’ cross-lingual priors. However, phonological\nand lexical heterogeneity across languages generally demands greater model capacity, whereas real-time ASR systems must operate under stringent latency and computational\nconstraints. This tension is reflected in deployment practice, where providers release multilingual-optimized variants,\n\nsuch as Seed-ASR (ML) [1] and Fun-ASR-MLT-nano [2],\nalongside their flagship models. The second direction introduces language-specific architectural mechanisms, such\nas language-aware adaptors, mixture-of-experts routing, and\ngated query banks [11, 12, 13]. While these methods can\nimprove multilingual performance at lower cost, they do not\ndirectly enhance the model’s underlying acoustic or semantic modeling capacity and may incur additional deployment\noverhead under high-concurrency inference.\nMulti-teacher on-policy distillation. On-policy distillation\n(OPD) [14] has recently emerged as an effective LLM posttraining algorithm, providing token-level supervision on the\nstudent’s sampled trajectories to mitigate exposure mismatch.\nThis property makes OPD well suited to LLM-based ASR\nsystems, which perform autoregressive generation yet require strict token-level transcription accuracy. Multi-teacher\nOPD [4, 5] extends this idea by independently optimizing\nmultiple domain-specialized teachers and distilling them into\nthe student model with reverse-KL-style objectives, making\nit suitable for capability integration. This is particularly appealing for multilingual ASR, as different languages rely on\ndistinct acoustic-phonetic perception and lexical-semantic\nmodeling capabilities. To the best of our knowledge, we are\nthe first to introduce MOPD into the ASR domain.\n3. METHODS\n3.1. Language-Specialized RL Teacher Training\nPrior to conducting MOPD, we construct a suite of teacher\nmodels specialized for distinct language categories. Starting\nfrom a base model πθ0 built upon the encoder–adaptor–LLM\narchitecture, we perform reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) based on DAPO [8]. For a given utterance x with reference transcription y, the rollout policy πθold\nsamples G = 8 hypotheses, {τi }G\ni=1 ∼ πθold (· | x). Fol-\n\n\fJDAPO (θ) = E \n\ni=1\n\nwhere ri,t = πθ (τi,t | x, τi,<t )/πθold (τi,t | x, τi,<t ) is the\ntoken-level importance ratio, and rei,t = clip(ri,t , 1−εlow , 1+\nεhigh ) is its asymmetrically clipped counterpart. Here, εlow\nand εhigh denote the clipping margins. Compared with classic RLVR objectives, DAPO uses a token-level objective to\nbalance contributions across transcription lengths and prevent\nerrors in long utterances from being underweighted. Its dynamic sampling strategy further improves efficiency by resampling informative instances.\nWe construct a fixed multilingual pool of 50k utterances:\n20k Mandarin, 10k Mandarin subdialects, 10k Cantonese, and\n10k English samples. We group these data into three routing\ncategories: Mandarin, Chinese dialects (Mandarin subdialects and Cantonese), and English. Each specialist teacher\nis trained on all data from its target routing category together\nwith a randomly sampled 20% subset from each of the other\nrouting categories, yielding three language-specialized teachers. In addition, a generalist teacher is trained on the full\npool. For controlled comparison, DAPO training varies the\nacoustic front end along two orthogonal dimensions: encoder\nmode (streaming vs. offline) and encoder-adaptor trainability (frozen vs. trainable), resulting in four configurations:\n{streaming, offline} × {frozen, trainable}.\n3.2. Language-Routed Multi-teacher OPD\nAfter obtaining the three language-specialized teachers and\nthe generalist teacher, we assess all teachers on held-out validation sets and rank them separately for Mandarin, Chinese\ndialects (including Mandarin subdialects and Cantonese), and\nEnglish. Let πϕTℓ(x),k denote the k-th ranked teacher for routing category ℓ(x), where k = 1 denotes the best-performing\nteacher. The MOPD student πθ is initialized from the base\nmodel πθ0 . Following MOPD [5], the student generates an\non-policy trajectory τ ∼ πθ (· | x). Let st = (x, τ<t ) denote\n\n(3)\nand the final MOPD objective aggregates contributions from\nthe top-K teachers (K = 3 by default):\n\"K\n#\nK\nX\nX\nLMOPD (θ) = Ex∼D\nλk Lk (x; θ) ,\nλk = 1. (4)\nk=1\n\nk=1\n\nThe weights {λk } govern the relative influence of each\nranked teacher, while setting λk = 0 allows the effective\nnumber of active teachers to range from three to one. The\nformulation recovers top-1 teacher distillation (see Fig. 1\n(a)) by setting (λ1 , λ2 , λ3 ) = (1, 0, 0), while also supporting\nweighted multi-teacher aggregation such as (0.6, 0.2, 0.2).\nImportantly, the supervising teacher πϕTℓ(x),k is determined\naccording to the evaluation performance of all candidate\nteachers for the language category ℓ(x). This ensures that\neach student trajectory can be supervised by the empirically\nstrongest teachers for its target language category.\n3.3. Preliminary Analysis on the Effect of Acoustic Prefix\n0.9550\n\n92\n\n0.82\n\n0.9500\n\n91\n\n0.80\n\n0.9475\n\n90\n\n0.78\n\n0.9450\nStatic prefix (Frozen)\nDynamic prefix (Trainable)\n\n0.9425\n0.9400\n\n93\n\n0.84\n\n0.9525\n\n0\n\n2\n\n4\n\n89\n\n0.76\n\n6\n\nTraining step (×10³)\n(a) Train reward during teacher training\n\nTokens with a positive OPD–CE\ngradient dot product (%) ↑\n\n\u0010\n\u0011\nbi , rei,t A\nbi\nmin\nr\nA\ni,t\nt=1\n , (2)\nPG\ni=1 |τi |\n\n PG P|τ |\ni\n\n\n|τ |\n\u0011\n\u0010\nX\n1\nLk (x; θ) = Eτ ∼πθ \nDKL πθ (· | st ) ∥ πϕTℓ(x),k (· | st )  ,\n|τ | t=1\n\n\nMean cosine similarity between\nOPD and CE logit gradients ↑\n\nwhere ℓ(x) denotes the language category of the input and\nERℓ(x) denotes the language-dependent transcription error\nrate, instantiated as character error rate (CER) for Chinese and\nword error rate (WER) for English. The group-normalized\nbi = (Ri − R)/(σR + ε),\nadvantage estimate is given by A\nwhere R and σR are the mean and standard deviation of the\nrewards within the rollout group, respectively. Groups with\nσR = 0 are discarded through dynamic sampling. DAPO\nthen optimizes the following policy-gradient objective:\n\nthe decoding state at step t. The distillation loss from the k-th\nranked teacher is defined as\n\nTrain reward ↑\n\nlowing the reward design adopted in [10], each hypothesis is\nscored by an accuracy reward:\n\u0002\n\u0003\nRi = Racc (τi , y) = exp −2 · ERℓ(x) (τi , y) ,\n(1)\n\nTop-1\n\nTop-2\n\nTop-3\n\n88\n\nNumber of teachers in the OPD target\n(b) OPD supervision aligns with the GT direction\n\nFig. 2. Comparison of static and dynamic acoustic-prefix configurations in terms of (a) accuracy reward trajectories during\nRL and (b) OPD–CE gradient alignment metrics during distillation. Error bars are estimated from 10k bootstrap resamples.\nWhen adapting MOPD to ASR, the LLM is conditioned\non acoustic embeddings that serve as acoustic prefixes, whose\nconfiguration directly influences distillation effectiveness. A\nstatic acoustic-prefix configuration ensures prefix consistency\nbetween teachers and the student, mitigating the teacher–\nstudent conditioning mismatch during distillation. However,\nthis configuration requires the teacher and student to share\na frozen encoder–adaptor throughout RL and OPD, which\nlimits the attainable performance of teachers. In contrast,\na dynamic configuration allows unrestricted joint optimization of all model components, but at the risk of increased\nconditioning inconsistency.\nTo illustrate this trade-off, we conduct a preliminary analysis under static and dynamic acoustic-prefix configurations.\n\n\fTable 1. Offline ASR performance measured by CER (%) for\nChinese and WER (%) for English (lower is better). Model\nsizes are shown in brackets.\nWeNet KeSpeech WeNet-Yue LibriSpeech\nAvg.\nmeet. | net\ntest\nshort | long clean | other\n\nMethod\nAdvanced Open-source Models\n[0.8B] Fun-ASR-Nano [2]\n[2.0B] Qwen3-ASR-1.7B [3]\n[8B] Step-Audio2-mini [15]\n[30B/A3B] Qwen3-Omni-Inst [16]\n[8B] MiMo-V2.5-ASR [17]\nOur Methods\n[2.3B] Base Model (NIM4-ASR)\nwith static acoustic prefix\nRL (Generalist Teacher)\nRL (Best-Teacher Oracle)\nLS-MOPD with 1 teacher\nLS-MOPD with 2 teachers\nLS-MOPD with 3 teachers\nwith dynamic acoustic prefix\nRL (Generalist Teacher)\nRL (Best-Teacher Oracle)\nLS-MOPD with 1 teacher\nLS-MOPD with 2 teachers\nLS-MOPD with 3 teachers\n\n4.68 | 5.22\n4.00 | 4.13\n4.23 | 4.63\n3.92 | 3.85\n4.63 | 4.52\n\n7.18\n4.98\n3.98\n5.96\n7.76\n\n7.31 | 10.02\n5.79 | 8.00\n7.78 | 8.44\n6.97 | 8.60\n6.53 | 10.52\n\n1.63 | 4.35\n1.56 | 3.49\n1.22 | 2.61\n1.15 | 2.38\n1.35 | 3.42\n\n5.77\n4.56\n4.70\n4.69\n5.53\n\n5.01 | 4.80\n\n4.57\n\n5.22 | 9.48\n\n1.23 | 2.61\n\n4.70\n\n4.88 | 4.74\n4.77 | 4.70\n4.62 | 4.53\n4.60 | 4.52\n4.60 | 4.53\n\n4.40\n4.40\n4.06\n4.13\n4.08\n\n5.27 | 9.49\n5.19 | 9.30\n5.19 | 9.12\n5.17 | 9.16\n5.14 | 9.14\n\n1.18 | 2.58\n1.18 | 2.56\n1.15 | 2.57\n1.15 | 2.53\n1.12 | 2.56\n\n4.65\n4.59\n4.46\n4.47\n4.45\n\n4.56 | 4.69\n4.56 | 4.69\n4.60 | 4.55\n4.49 | 4.58\n4.52 | 4.62\n\n4.49\n4.45\n4.14\n4.13\n4.12\n\n5.35 | 9.55\n5.25 | 9.19\n5.28 | 9.15\n5.23 | 9.16\n5.17 | 9.27\n\n1.20 | 2.57\n1.17 | 2.56\n1.14 | 2.56\n1.17 | 2.48\n1.15 | 2.51\n\n4.63\n4.55\n4.49\n4.46\n4.48\n\nNote: Boldface separately indicates the best result among “Advanced\nOpen-source Models” and among “Our Methods” for each benchmark.\n\nAs shown in Fig. 2, we compare (a) the accuracy-reward\ntrajectories during RL training and (b) token-level alignment\nbetween the OPD gradients and the cross-entropy (CE) gradients derived from ground-truth labels during distillation.\nThe latter is quantified by the mean cosine similarity and\nthe proportion of directionally aligned tokens, defined as\nthose with a positive OPD–CE gradient dot product. The\nresults clearly show that the static prefix yields slightly lower\nteacher performance but better-aligned distillation gradients,\nwhereas the dynamic prefix improves teacher performance\nat the expense of slightly reduced gradient alignment. This\nreveals a trade-off between teacher strength and distillation\ncompatibility, further examined in the following section.\n\nTable 2. Streaming ASR performance measured by CER (%)\nfor Chinese and WER (%) for English (lower is better).\nMethod\nBase Model (NIM4-ASR)\nwith static acoustic prefix\nRL (Generalist Teacher)\nRL (Best-Teacher Oracle)\nLS-MOPD with 1 teacher\nLS-MOPD with 2 teachers\nLS-MOPD with 3 teachers\nwith dynamic acoustic prefix\nRL (Generalist Teacher)\nRL (Best-Teacher Oracle)\nLS-MOPD with 1 teacher\nLS-MOPD with 2 teachers\nLS-MOPD with 3 teachers\n\nWeNet KeSpeech WeNet-Yue LibriSpeech\nAvg.\nmeet. | net\ntest\nshort | long clean | other\n6.19 | 5.26\n\n5.69\n\n6.06 | 12.33 1.34 | 3.32\n\n5.74\n\n5.92 | 5.19\n5.84 | 5.18\n5.76 | 5.00\n5.67 | 4.99\n5.69 | 4.96\n\n5.54\n5.54\n5.12\n5.17\n5.09\n\n5.68 | 11.10\n5.50 | 11.10\n5.61 | 10.83\n5.59 | 10.67\n5.52 | 10.61\n\n1.29 | 3.27\n1.25 | 3.20\n1.25 | 3.21\n1.24 | 3.15\n1.23 | 3.19\n\n5.43\n5.37\n5.25\n5.21\n5.18\n\n5.90 | 5.26\n5.72 | 5.26\n5.65 | 5.17\n5.71 | 5.16\n5.78 | 5.26\n\n5.52\n5.50\n5.30\n5.24\n5.22\n\n5.60 | 11.10\n5.58 | 10.96\n5.58 | 11.19\n5.99 | 11.02\n5.51 | 10.90\n\n1.35 | 3.22\n1.31 | 3.16\n1.29 | 3.17\n1.27 | 3.12\n1.28 | 3.15\n\n5.42\n5.36\n5.34\n5.36\n5.30\n\naforementioned four datasets, comprising seven subsets in\ntotal. Across all evaluations, a unified text normalization\npipeline based on WenetTextProcess is applied to mitigate\nthe effects of surface-form variations. All experiments adopt\ndeterministic decoding strategies. In the streaming setting,\nspeech is encoded in 640 ms chunks, with four preceding\nchunks retained as left context, and greedy decoding (beam\nsize = 1) is employed. In the offline setting, the encoder operates over the complete utterance, and beam search decoding\nis applied with a beam size of 3.\n4.2. Training Details\nFor RL training, we train for at most 6k optimization steps\nwith a maximum learning rate of 2 × 10−6 linearly decayed\nto zero, and a sampling temperature that is cosine-annealed\nfrom 1.0 to 0.7. For MOPD, we train for 20k steps using a\nmaximum learning rate of 4 × 10−6 cosine-annealed to 2 ×\n10−6 and a fixed sampling temperature of 1.0. All RL and\nMOPD experiments are conducted on 8 NVIDIA A100 GPUs\nwith BF16 precision and DeepSpeed ZeRO-2 [22].\n\n4. EXPERIMENTS\n4.3. Main Results\n4.1. Experimental Setup\nThe base student model in this work is built upon an inhouse backbone pretrained on approximately 560k hours of\nlabeled speech, comprising a 0.6B FireRed Conformer [9], a\n4× downsampling linear adaptor, and a Qwen3-1.7B LLM\ndecoder. For RL and MOPD, we construct a 50k-utterance\nmultilingual training pool by sampling from representative\ndatasets: 20k Mandarin utterances from WenetSpeech [18],\n10k Mandarin subdialect utterances from KeSpeech [19],\n10k Cantonese utterances from WenetSpeech-Yue [20], and\n10k English utterances from LibriSpeech [21]. The generalist teacher and MOPD stages use the full multilingual pool,\nwhereas language-specialized teachers are trained on their respective language-skewed subsets constructed with sampling\nratios specified in Section 3.1.\nFor evaluation, we employ the official test sets of the\n\nTables 1 and 2 report the performance of our proposed methods on benchmarks, alongside leading open-source models\nas references for the current state of the art. Within each\nacoustic-prefix configuration, the generalist teacher serves as\nthe RL baseline, whereas the best result attained by any candidate teacher on each benchmark is reported as “RL (BestTeacher Oracle)”. For LS-MOPD, the number of supervising teachers is determined by the loss weights in Eq. (4):\nthe one-, two-, and three-teacher variants use LMOPD = L1 ,\nLMOPD = 0.6L1 + 0.4L2 , and LMOPD = 0.6L1 + 0.2L2 +\n0.2L3 , respectively. We analyze the results as follows:\n(1) Overall Performance. Using only a 50k-utterance\npost-training pool, LS-MOPD substantially improves upon\nthe base model and consistently outperforms the RL baseline.\nThe average error rate drops to a minimum of 4.45%, outperforming all open-source baselines, including models trained\n\n\f4.4. Sources of Improvement and Emergent Gains\nBeyond benchmark results, we conduct visual analyses to validate LS-MOPD and offer insight into its emergent behavior.\nAs shown in Fig. 3 (a), we examine the relationship between the benchmark gains of different LS-MOPD variants\nand the proportion of tokens with aligned OPD and CE gradients, as defined in Section 3.3. The results show a positive\nassociation: static-prefix LS-MOPD variants exhibit more\nconsistently aligned gradients and achieve larger performance\ngains. By contrast, although dynamic-prefix teachers show\nstronger standalone performance, their supervision induces\ngradients that are less aligned with the CE gradients, leading\nto smaller distillation gains. Moreover, multi-teacher supervision generally yields better gradient alignment than singleteacher supervision. This finding provides a preliminary\n\n0.15\n\nStatic prefix (Frozen)\nDynamic prefix (Trainable)\n\n0.10\n0.05\n0.00\n\nTop-3\n\nTop-3\nTop-2\n\nTop-1\n\nTop-1\nTop-2\n90.0\n\n90.5\n\n91.0\n\n91.5\n\nStudent correct while all teachers\nare wrong (% of reference tokens) ↑\n\nError-rate reduction relative to\nthe dataset-best teacher (points) ↑\n\non million-hour-scale speech corpora [2, 3] and those built\nupon larger LLM backbones [15, 16, 17], using only 2.3B parameters. Notably, LS-MOPD surpasses the empirical performance envelope defined by the best-teacher oracle on almost\nall benchmarks, and achieves the best results among all compared leading systems on WeNetSpeech-Yue-short and LibriSpeech test-clean. These results demonstrate that the distilled student can outperform all teachers, achieving highly\ncompetitive multilingual recognition accuracy together with\nmeasurable emergent gains. As shown by the streaming results in Table 2, LS-MOPD also delivers substantial gains in\nthe streaming setting, with relative improvements exceeding\nthose observed in the offline configuration.\n(2) Static vs. dynamic acoustic prefix. Before distillation, dynamic-prefix RL teachers achieve slightly better\naverage performance than their static-prefix counterparts,\nowing to joint encoder–adaptor optimization that yields more\nrefined acoustic representations. After MOPD, however,\nstatic-prefix variants attain the best average results in both the\noffline and streaming settings. We attribute this reversal to\nteacher–student acoustic-prefix mismatch under the dynamic\nconfiguration: when teacher supervision depends on acoustic cues absent from the student’s representation, distillation\nmay shift from learnable preference transfer toward fitting\npartially unreachable targets.\n(3) Top-K teacher weighted aggregation. Since crosslingual heterogeneity is generally less pronounced than crosstask heterogeneity, we extend the default top-1 routing of\nMOPD to weighted top-K teacher aggregation. This yields\nmodest gains, particularly in the streaming setting, suggesting a beneficial regularization effect that becomes more pronounced under higher decoding uncertainty. Notably, we\nfind that incorporating the second- and third-ranked teachers\ncan lead to improvements despite their substantially lower\nstandalone performance than the best teacher, suggesting that\nteacher diversity can provide complementary supervision and\nalleviate token-level overconfidence.\n\nTokens with a positive OPD–CE gradient dot product (%) ↑\n\n0.30\n0.25\n0.20\n0.15\n0.10\n0.05\n0.00\nTop-1\n\nTop-2\n\nTop-3\n\nNumber of teachers in the OPD target\n\n(a) Helpful-gradient prevalence is associated with error-rate gain\n\n(b) Student-only correct positions\n\nFig. 3. (a) Relationship between OPD–CE gradient alignment\nand the performance gains achieved by LS-MOPD. (b) Emergent gains: the proportion of tokens incorrectly predicted by\nall teachers but correctly predicted by the student.\nmechanistic explanation for why multi-teacher distillation\ncan outperform its single-teacher counterpart: lower-ranked\nteachers can still contribute complementary supervision that\ninduces more favorable optimization directions.\nFig. 3 (b) further reveals the emergent benefit of LSMOPD: a non-negligible proportion of tokens are predicted\nincorrectly by all teachers, yet are correctly recognized by the\nstudent. Together with the consistent improvements of LSMOPD over the best-teacher oracle in Tables 1 and 2, these\nresults highlight the potential of LS-MOPD for multilingual\nASR, demonstrating that coordinated supervision from multiple teachers can enable the student to generalize beyond the\nperformance envelope of any individual teacher.\n5. CONCLUSION\nThis work proposes a multi-teacher on-policy distillation\nframework for multilingual LLM-based ASR. We systematically investigate the impact of static and dynamic acousticprefix configurations and integrate language-specialized\nteachers through language-routed, weighted supervision. Experimental results across both offline and streaming scenarios\ndemonstrate that LS-MOPD substantially outperforms RL\nbaselines and has the potential to generalize beyond the empirical upper envelope of trained RL teachers. We further\nanalyze the effectiveness of LS-MOPD from the perspectives\nof gradient alignment and multi-teacher complementarity,\nand derive practical insights into acoustic-prefix selection\nand multi-teacher aggregation.\nOur work also has several limitations. First, the evaluation\nis relatively narrow in scope, covering only a single model\nbackbone and a limited set of target languages. Broader validation across model architectures and languages is still warranted. Second, our current dynamic acoustic-prefix configuration remains preliminary and does not yet fully exploit the\npotential of this design. Future work will explore teacher–\nstudent supervision mechanisms that preserve the optimization flexibility of dynamic acoustic prefixes while reducing\nthe resulting conditioning mismatch.\n\n\f6. REFERENCES\n[1] Ye Bai, Jingping Chen, Jitong Chen, Wei Chen, Zhuo\nChen, Chuang Ding, Linhao Dong, Qianqian Dong, Yujiao Du, Kepan Gao, et al., “Seed-asr: Understanding diverse speech and contexts with llm-based speech recognition,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.04675, 2024.\n[2] Keyu An, Yanni Chen, Zhigao Chen, Chong Deng, Zhihao Du, Changfeng Gao, Zhifu Gao, Bo Gong, Xiangang Li, Yabin Li, et al., “Fun-asr technical report,”\narXiv preprint arXiv:2509.12508, 2025.\n[3] Xian Shi, Xiong Wang, Zhifang Guo, Yongqi Wang,\nPei Zhang, Xinyu Zhang, Zishan Guo, Hongkun Hao,\nYu Xi, Baosong Yang, et al., “Qwen3-asr technical report,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.21337, 2026.\n[4] Bangjun Xiao, Bingquan Xia, Bo Yang, Bofei Gao,\nBowen Shen, Chen Zhang, Chenhong He, Chiheng Lou,\nFuli Luo, Gang Wang, et al., “Mimo-v2-flash technical\nreport,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.02780, 2026.\n[5] Wenhan Ma, Jianyu Wei, Liang Zhao, Hailin Zhang,\nBangjun Xiao, Lei Li, Qibin Yang, Bofei Gao, Yudong\nWang, Rang Li, Jinhao Dong, Zhifang Sui, and Fuli\nLuo, “Mopd: Multi-teacher on-policy distillation for capability integration in llm post-training,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2606.30406, 2026.\n[6] Zhuolin Yang, Zihan Liu, Yang Chen, Wenliang Dai,\nBoxin Wang, Sheng-Chieh Lin, Chankyu Lee, Yangyi\nChen, Dongfu Jiang, Jiafan He, et al., “Nemotroncascade 2: Post-training llms with cascade rl and\nmulti-domain on-policy distillation,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2603.19220, 2026.\n[7] Anyi Xu, Bangcai Lin, Bing Xue, Bingxuan Wang,\nBingzheng Xu, Bochao Wu, Bowei Zhang, Chaofan\nLin, Chen Dong, Chenchen Ling, et al., “Deepseekv4: Towards highly efficient million-token context intelligence,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.19348, 2026.\n[8] Qiying Yu, Zheng Zhang, Ruofei Zhu, Yufeng Yuan, Xiaochen Zuo, Yu Yue, Weinan Dai, Tiantian Fan, Gaohong Liu, Lingjun Liu, et al., “Dapo: An open-source\nllm reinforcement learning system at scale,” Advances\nin Neural Information Processing Systems, vol. 38, pp.\n113222–113244, 2025.\n[9] Kaituo Xu, Yan Jia, Kai Huang, Junjie Chen, Wenpeng Li, Kun Liu, Feng-Long Xie, Xu Tang, and Yao\nHu, “Fireredasr2s: A state-of-the-art industrial-grade\nall-in-one automatic speech recognition system,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2603.10420, 2026.\n\n[10] Yuan Xie, Jiaqi Song, Guang Qiu, Xianliang Wang, Kai\nQiao, Junfeng Yuan, Shengqing Liu, Yi Zhang, Bowen\nChen, Ming Lei, et al., “Nim4-asr: Towards efficient,\nrobust, and customizable real-time llm-based asr,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2604.18105, 2026.\n[11] Junjie Li, Jing Peng, Yangui Fang, Shuai Wang, and Kai\nYu, “Mosa: Mixtures of simple adapters outperform\nmonolithic approaches in llm-based multilingual asr,”\nin 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\nSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2026,\npp. 18822–18826.\n[12] Guodong Lin, Ziqi Chen, Yuxiang Fu, Ke Li, and WeiQiang Zhang, “Enhancing multilingual llm-based asr\nwith mixture of experts and dynamic downsampling,”\nin 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\nSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2026,\npp. 18807–18811.\n[13] Shreyas Gopal, Donghang Wu, Ashutosh Anshul,\nYeo Yue Heng, Yizhou Peng, Haoyang Li, Hexin Liu,\nand Eng Siong Chng, “Language-aware distillation for\nmultilingual instruction-following speech llms with asronly supervision,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.07025,\n2026.\n[14] Rishabh Agarwal, Nino Vieillard, Yongchao Zhou, Piotr Stanczyk, Sabela Ramos Garea, Matthieu Geist, and\nOlivier Bachem, “On-policy distillation of language\nmodels: Learning from self-generated mistakes,” in\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations,\n2024.\n[15] Boyong Wu, Chao Yan, Chen Hu, Cheng Yi, Chengli\nFeng, Fei Tian, Feiyu Shen, Gang Yu, Haoyang Zhang,\nJingbei Li, et al., “Step-audio 2 technical report,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2507.16632, 2025.\n[16] Jin Xu, Zhifang Guo, Hangrui Hu, Yunfei Chu, Xiong\nWang, Jinzheng He, Yuxuan Wang, Xian Shi, Ting He,\nXinfa Zhu, et al., “Qwen3-omni technical report,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2509.17765, 2025.\n[17] LLM-Core-Team Xiaomi, “Mimo-v2.5-asr: Robust\nspeech recognition across languages, dialects, and complex acoustic scenarios,” 2026.\n[18] Binbin Zhang, Hang Lv, Pengcheng Guo, Qijie Shao,\nChao Yang, Lei Xie, Xin Xu, Hui Bu, Xiaoyu Chen,\nChenchen Zeng, et al., “Wenetspeech: A 10000+ hours\nmulti-domain mandarin corpus for speech recognition,”\nin 2022 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\nSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2022,\npp. 6182–6186.\n\n\f[19] Zhiyuan Tang, Dong Wang, Yanguang Xu, Jianwei Sun